The function is open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says, 'Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first { to the last }: reasoning prose before or after the answer frequently contains stray braces, and an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own.' It also says, 'A candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable; one that does not (a float, for instance) is passed over.' The two replies are Text A: '"{"a":1}"' (a nine-character string: double quote, left brace, double quote, a, double quote, colon, 1, right brace, double quote) and Text B: '{"a":1}' (a seven-character string: left brace, double quote, a, double quote, colon, 1, right brace). For Text A, the only balanced object is inside a string literal, so it is not a top-level candidate; the rule requires recover_json_object to raise RecordError('no JSON object could be recovered from the response'). For Text B, the object is top-level and parses as strict JSON, so the rule requires recover_json_object to return ({'a': 1}, ()). The check gives the same answer for both: it returns ({'a': 1}, ()) for Text A as well, because its top-level scan uses raw_decode at the left brace and does not notice that the brace is inside a string. I would be wrong if recover_json_object('"{"a":1}"') actually raises RecordError, or if the documented rule is read to count an object inside a string literal as a top-level candidate.